+Afterwards, a current NDT sample is not required for each GNSS update: NDT and
+GNSS remain independent observations of the same EKF, so an NDT outage does not
+silently become a dual-sensor outage. The default remains `official`; this policy
+requires separate validation before product use.
+
+For deployments where that EKF output is also fed back as the next scan-matcher
+initial guess, `gnss_require_fresh_time_aligned_ndt` can be enabled. In that
+explicit mode, a GNSS position is accepted only while a valid NDT observation is
+fresh in receive time and close in source time. This prevents a bad GNSS stream
+from continuing as a de-facto GNSS-only localization source after it has already
+caused the matcher to stop producing valid measurements. The option is disabled
+by default, so upstream-compatible behavior is unchanged.
 
 `ndt_primary_position` also exposes disabled-by-default consistency gates:
 `gnss_ndt_max_stamp_delta_sec` and `gnss_ndt_xy_innovation_max_m`.
@@ -48,6 +56,39 @@
 mistaking normal vehicle motion between asynchronous measurements for spatial
 disagreement; it does not alter either measurement published to the EKF.
 
+For covariance-aware integrity monitoring,
+`gnss_ndt_cross_source_nis_soft_gate` and
+`gnss_ndt_cross_source_nis_hard_gate` evaluate the horizontal normalized
+innovation squared (NIS) with `S = R_gnss + R_ndt`. The hard gate rejects a
+statistically inconsistent GNSS update. Between the soft and hard gates, only
+the GNSS horizontal covariance is inflated by `NIS / soft_gate`; the GNSS
+position and every NDT field remain unchanged. Both gates are disabled by
+default and must be enabled together with `0 < soft < hard`.
+
+`gnss_position_updates_enabled=false` is an experiment/control switch for
+`ndt_primary_position`: GNSS messages still traverse validation and integrity
+monitoring, but are not published into the EKF. This permits a paired NDT-only
+comparison with the same modifier, subscriptions, executor and single EKF. It
+does not change the upstream-compatible `official` default.
+
+For a bounded LiDAR outage,
+`gnss_ndt_trusted_outage_bridge_enabled` provides a disabled-by-default
+integrity holdover. It is not GNSS-only initialization and does not create
+another estimator: GNSS must first agree with fresh, source-time-aligned NDT
+for a configured number of observations in a bounded recent window. By default
+every sample in the window must agree, preserving the historical consecutive
+rule; `gnss_ndt_trusted_outage_minimum_agreement_count` can explicitly permit a
+small, fixed number of soft outliers without permitting a hard conflict. Only then may that same
+continuous GNSS stream keep updating the one EKF while NDT is absent for a
+bounded duration. A source-time gap, a GNSS trajectory jump, an expired
+holdover, a delayed/reordered GNSS sample, or loss of prior cross-source
+agreement revokes the permission. GNSS covariance is inflated as the time
+since the last NDT corroboration grows. This cannot prove that one receiver can
+detect an arbitrarily slow common-mode bias, so combined LiDAR-loss/GNSS-bias
+cases remain mandatory safety tests.
+When `gnss_ndt_trusted_outage_agreement_nis` is positive, qualification uses
+the covariance-aware NIS instead of a fixed metre threshold.
+
 ## Assumptions
 
 - The NDT matcher provides a pose with a fixed covariance.
